    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer, so all devices participate in *every* layer's computation.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts* (whole FFN blocks), while attention and other dense layers are typically replicated (or sharded via TP/DP). Devices participate only in the layers where their experts are routed.\n\nIn practice, production systems combine these: dense layers use TP, MoE layers use EP (often with TP *within* an expert, i.e., \"expert-tensor parallelism\"), and data parallelism (DP) replicates the whole sharded model to scale batch size. This note focuses on the mechanics of TP and EP during **inference** (forward pass only, no gradients), including data flow, communication collectives, performance characteristics, and correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention: $Y = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V \\cdot W_O$\n- MLP: $Y = \\text{act}(X W_1) W_2$\n\nMegatron-style TP shards as follows:\n\n| Component | Sharding | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ | Column-parallel (split output dim) | Attention heads are independent; splitting by head count gives each rank whole heads |\n| $W_O$ | Row-parallel (split input dim) |",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
